  "before_interfaces": {
    "prop-ip-posterior-mean-is-square-loss-optimal": [
      "## Statement\n\nAssume the Axiom of Choice. Let $(\\mathsf Y,\\mathcal Y)$ be a measurable data space, let $H$ be a separable real Hilbert space, and fix a complete orthonormal basis $(e_j)_{j\\in J}$, where $J$ is either finite with its supplied ordering or $J=\\mathbb N$. A complex Hilbert signal space is included by viewing it as a real Hilbert space with inner product the real part of its complex inner product and supplying a real orthonormal basis. Let $\\Pi$ be a probability kernel from $\\mathsf Y$ to $H$ with its norm Borel sigma-algebra. Define\n$$D_2:=\\left\\{y\\in\\mathsf Y:\\int_H\\|h\\|_H^2\\,\\Pi_y(dh)<\\infty\\right\\}.$$\nFor every $y\\in D_2$ and $j\\in J$, define\n$$m_j(y):=\\int_H\\langle h,e_j\\rangle_H\\,\\Pi_y(dh),\\qquad \\mu(y):=\\sum_{j\\in J}m_j(y)e_j,$$\nwhere the last sum is the norm limit of its finite partial sums. Then $D_2\\in\\mathcal Y$, the sum defining $\\mu(y)$ exists for every $y\\in D_2$, and $\\mu:D_2\\to H$ is measurable. For every $y\\in D_2$ and every action $a\\in H$,\n$$\\int_H\\|h-a\\|_H^2\\,\\Pi_y(dh)=\\int_H\\|h-\\mu(y)\\|_H^2\\,\\Pi_y(dh)+\\|a-\\mu(y)\\|_H^2.$$\nThus $\\mu(y)$ is the unique finite-risk action minimizing posterior squared-error risk over all of $H$, and it is a measurable Bayes action rule on $D_2$. The minimization is over the full Hilbert space: the mean need not belong to a nonconvex admissible signal subset, and no perceptual-representativeness claim is made. For a probability space $(\\Omega,\\mathcal F,P)$, let $Y:\\Omega\\to(\\mathsf Y,\\mathcal Y)$ and $T:\\Omega\\to H$ be measurable random elements with $E\\|T\\|_H^2<\\infty$. If the same $\\Pi$ is a supplied regular conditional law of $T$ given $Y$, write $P_Y(B)=P(Y\\in B)$ for the law of $Y$. Then $D_2$ has full $P_Y$-measure. Extend $\\mu$ by zero to a measurable map $\\bar\\mu:\\mathsf Y\\to H$; the coordinates of $\\bar\\mu(Y)$ are versions of $E[\\langle T,e_j\\rangle_H\\mid\\sigma(Y)]$, and $\\bar\\mu(Y)$ is square-integrable. Any square-integrable $H$-valued random variable whose basis coordinates are versions of those same conditional expectations equals $\\bar\\mu(Y)$ almost surely. The RCD's values on $P_Y$-null data sets remain version choices."
    ],
    "def-ip-learned-reconstruction-estimator": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $(X,d_X)$ and $(Y,d_Y)$ be metric parameter and data spaces, let $U\\subseteq X$ be a nonempty admissible truth class, let $F:U\\to Y$ be a forward map, and let $(Q,d_Q)$ be a metric target space with a measurable target map $q:U\\to Q$. The target $q(x)$ is the recovery object assigned to truth $x$; $Q$ may be the parameter space itself or a quotient solution-class space. All metric spaces in this definition carry their Borel sigma-algebras. A **point reconstruction rule** is a Borel map $g:Y\\to Q$, and its output at data $y$ is $g(y)$. A **learned point estimator** is such a map $\\widehat q_\\phi$ with a fitted parameter $\\phi$ in a specified parameter set. A complete training description names the probability law $P_{\\rm fit}$ of all variables used to fit $\\phi$; those variables may be paired or unpaired. A complete test description names a probability law $P_{\\rm test}$ for evaluation pairs $(Y,q(X))$. No equality between fitting and test laws is assumed.\n\nA finite **unrolled learned update** consists of an integer $K\\geq1$, a Borel initialization $u_0:Y\\to Q$, and Borel maps $T_{\\phi,k}:Q\\times Y\\to Q$ for $0\\leq k<K$, with $u_{k+1}(y)=T_{\\phi,k}(u_k(y),y)$. Its output $u_K(y)$ is a point reconstruction rule. An **amortized inverse map** is a learned point estimator applied to multiple data inputs after a single fit; this term adds a reuse convention, not an accuracy guarantee.\n\nFor a real Hilbert signal space $H$, a **denoiser** is a Borel map $D_\\phi:H\\to H$ trained under a specified signal-corruption law and used to transform a corrupted signal into a signal estimate or an update. A denoiser is the exact minimum mean-square error map only when it equals the conditional mean for the specified signal and corruption laws. A **learned prior** is a supplied Borel probability measure $\\rho_\\phi$ on $H$; one construction is the pushforward law $\\rho_\\phi(A)=\\nu(G_\\phi^{-1}(A))$ of a specified probability $\\nu$ under a Borel generator $G_\\phi:Z\\to H$. The law or generator alone is not a data-to-target estimator.\n\nIn a finite-dimensional signal space $\\mathbb R^d$, a **score field model** is a Borel map $s_\\phi:[0,T]\\times\\mathbb R^d\\to\\mathbb R^d$ intended to approximate $\\nabla_x\\log p_t(x)$ for a specified family of positive continuously differentiable densities $p_t$. A score field is not itself a posterior law, posterior sample, or point reconstruction rule. A **posterior sampler** is a probability kernel $S:Y\\to Q$; relative to a supplied posterior target kernel $\\Pi_y$, it is exact on a data domain $D$ when $S_y=\\Pi_y$ as probability measures for every $y\\in D$. A draw from $S_y$ is random; obtaining a point estimate from it requires an additional summary rule. The name “learned” by itself asserts no exact recovery, stability, convergence, calibration, or train-to-test guarantee."
    ],
    "def-ip-inverse-risk-under-train-and-test-laws": [
      "## Definition\n\nLet $(\\mathsf Y,\\mathcal Y)$ and $(Q,\\mathcal Q)$ be measurable data and target spaces, with $\\mathcal Q$ the Borel sigma-algebra of the target metric space. Let $P$ be a probability measure on the product space $(\\mathsf Y\\times Q,\\mathcal Y\\otimes\\mathcal Q)$, and let $\\ell:Q\\times Q\\to[0,+\\infty]$ be product-measurable, where $\\ell(t,a)$ is the loss for target $t$ and action $a$. For a Borel point reconstruction rule $g:\\mathsf Y\\to Q$, its risk under $P$ is the extended nonnegative integral\n$$R_P(g):=\\int_{\\mathsf Y\\times Q}\\ell(t,g(y))\\,P(dy,dt).$$\nFor a randomized reconstruction rule given by a probability kernel $K$ from $\\mathsf Y$ to $Q$, define\n$$R_P(K):=\\int_{\\mathsf Y\\times Q}\\!\\left(\\int_Q\\ell(t,a)\\,K(y,da)\\right)P(dy,dt).$$\nThe inner integral is measurable in $(y,t)$ by kernel integration; a deterministic rule is the special case $K_y=\\delta_{g(y)}$. When $P_{\\rm train}$ and $P_{\\rm test}$ are specified joint laws on the same input-target space, the corresponding training-law risk and test-law risk are $R_{P_{\\rm train}}(K)$ and $R_{P_{\\rm test}}(K)$. Either value may be $+\\infty$. This definition imposes no inequality between the two risks: any such comparison requires an additional assumption relating the joint laws or the rule's behavior under them."
